Review record for the missouri install fix.

The change replaces one install command in the release workflow with the
action that already installs missouri on every pull request. No code, no
dependency, and no user-facing surface changes. The driver verified it
directly. Each line states what was checked.

signoff[review-tests] PASS edd5e52 no test reads a workflow file, so the release run is the check; the failing tag run named the exact error, "multiple packages with binaries found: missouri, myproject", and the workflow is dispatched as a dry run on this branch before any tag moves, which runs every build job and skips every publish job
signoff[review-code] PASS edd5e52 the action was read at cjohnhanson/missouri action.yml, and it runs `cargo install --git ... --rev <sha> --locked --root ~/.missouri-tools missouri`, which names the package cargo asked for and caches the result by sha; the step list of the verify job was printed from the edited file with a YAML reader, and the action sits before the suite run
signoff[review-docs] PASS edd5e52 the step carries a comment naming the cause, that a fixture in missouri is itself a package with a binary, and no document states the release workflow's install command, so nothing else contradicts it
signoff[review-deps] PASS edd5e52 no manifest and no lockfile changes; the action is the one the pull request workflow already runs, and it pins missouri to a resolved sha rather than a floating branch, which is a narrower dependency than the command it replaces
signoff[review-usability] PASS edd5e52 no binary, help text or error message changes; the reader affected is whoever cuts the next tag, and what they met was a red verify job whose error named both packages and the flag that disambiguates them
